== Chapter XI

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[Concerning effectual calling.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

#smallcaps[The] second qualification of the persons to whom this privilege in the text belongs is that they are #emph[the called of God:] all things work together for good τοῖς κλητοῖς, “to those who are called.” Though this word \[#emph[called]\] is placed after loving God in #emph[order,] yet in #emph[nature] it comes before it. Love is #emph[named] first, but it is not #emph[worked] first. We must be #emph[called] by God before we can #emph[love] God.

In verse 30 of this chapter, #emph[calling] is made the middle link of the golden chain of salvation. It is placed between predestination and glorification; and if we hold this middle link fast, we are sure of the two ends of the chain. To make this clearer, six things are to be observed.

=== Section I. A Distinction about Calling.

1\. I will set down a #emph[distinction.] There are two kinds of #emph[call.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. An #emph[outward call,] which is nothing else than God’s blessed offer of grace in the gospel, his pleading with sinners, when he woos them to come in and accept mercy. Our Savior speaks of this in Matt 20:16: “Many are called, but few are chosen.” This outward call is not enough for salvation, yet it is enough to leave men without excuse.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. There is an #emph[inward call,] when God wonderfully overpowers the heart and draws the will to embrace Christ. This is, as #emph[Augustine] puts it, #emph[vocatio alta, & efficax,] an #emph[effectual call.] By the outward call God blows a trumpet in the ear; by the inward call he opens the heart, as he opened the heart of #emph[Lydia] (Acts 16:14). The outward call may bring men to a #emph[profession] of Christ; the inward call brings them to a #emph[possession] of Christ. The outward call curbs a sinner; the inward call changes him.]]

=== Section II. Our Pitiful Condition before We Are Called.

2.#smallcaps[What] we are before this call. (1) We are in a state of slavery.#footnote[#emph[Nihil durius servitute.] \[Nothing is harder than slavery.\] Cicero.] Before God calls a man, he is at the devil’s call. If the devil says go, he goes. The deluded sinner is like the slave who digs in the #emph[mine,] cuts stone in the quarry, and pulls at the oar. He is at the command of Satan as the donkey is at the command of the driver. (2) We are in a state of darkness (Eph 5:8): “You#super[+] were once darkness.” First, darkness is very cheerless. A man in the dark is full of fear; he trembles at every step he takes. Second, it is dangerous. He who is in the dark may quickly go out of the right way and fall into rivers and whirlpools. So in the darkness of ignorance we may quickly fall into the whirlpool of hell. (3) Before we are savingly called, we are in a state of helplessness (Rom 5:6): ἡμῶν ἀσθενῶν, “while we were still powerless.” We have no strength to resist a temptation or to grapple with a corruption; sin #emph[cut the lock where our strength lay.] Indeed, there is not only helplessness but stubbornness (Acts 7:51): “You#super[+] always resist the Holy Spirit.” Besides an unwillingness to do good, there is opposition to it. (4) We are in a state of pollution (Ezek 16:6): “I … saw you polluted in your blood.” The imagination coins earthly thoughts; the heart is the devil’s forge, where the sparks of lust fly. (5) We are in a state of damnation. We are born under a sad planet (John 3:36): “The wrath of God remains on us.” This is our condition before God is pleased by a merciful #emph[call] to bring us near to himself and free us from the misery in which we were swallowed up before.

=== Section III. The Means of Our Blessed Call.

3\. #smallcaps[The] means of our #emph[effectual call.] The ordinary means the Lord uses in calling us is not raptures and revelations, as the #emph[Familists] hold, but is:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Partly by his #emph[word,] which is #emph[virga virtutis, the rod of his strength] (Ps 110:2). The voice of the word is God’s call to us. That is why he is said to speak to us \[#emph[now]\] from heaven (Heb 12:25), that is, in the ministry of the word. When the word calls us from sin, it is as if we heard a voice from heaven.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Partly by his #emph[Spirit;] this is the #emph[loud call.] The word is the #emph[instrumental] cause of our conversion; the Spirit is the #emph[efficient] cause. The ministers of God are only the pipes and organs. It is the Spirit blowing in them that effectually changes the heart (Acts 10:44): “While Peter was still speaking …, the Holy Spirit fell upon all who heard his message”#footnote[#emph[Nisi spiritus adsit cordi audientis, inanis est labor docentis.] \[Unless the Spirit is present in the heart of the hearer, the labor of the teacher is in vain.\]]. It is not the farmer’s hard work in plowing and sowing that will make the ground fruitful without the early and the latter rain. So the seed of the word will not effectually convert unless the Spirit puts forth his sweet influence and drops like rain on the heart. That is why we must pray for the help of God’s Spirit, that he would put forth his powerful voice and wake us out of the grave of unbelief. If a man knocks at a gate of brass, it will not open; but if he comes with a key in his hand, it will open. So when God, who has #emph[the key of David] in his hand#footnote[Rev 3:7.], comes, he opens the heart, however tightly it is locked against him.]]

=== Section IV. God’s Method in Calling Sinners.

4\. #smallcaps[The] method God uses in calling sinners. The Lord does not tie himself to one way, or use the same order with everyone. He comes:

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Sometimes in the #emph[still voice.] Those who have had godly parents and have sat under the warm sunshine of a religious upbringing often do not know #emph[how] or #emph[when] they were called. The Lord secretly and gradually put grace into their hearts, as the dew falls in drops without being felt. They know by the heavenly effects that they are called, but they do not know the time or the manner. The hand moves on the dial, but we do not notice when it moves. This is how God deals with some.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. Others are more stubborn and knotty sinners, and God comes to them in a #emph[rough wind.] He uses more wedges of the law to break their hearts. He humbles them deeply and shows them they are damned without Christ. Then, having plowed up the fallow ground of their hearts by humbling them, he sows the seeds of comfort. He sets Christ and mercy before them, and draws their wills not only to accept Christ, but to long for him passionately and to rest on him in trust. This is how he worked on #emph[Paul,] and called him from a persecutor to a preacher. This call is more visible than the other, yet not more real. An opening may be made in the body by a caustic as well as by an incision. God’s method in calling sinners may vary, but the effect is always the same.]]

=== Section V. The Properties of the Divine Call.

5\. #smallcaps[The] properties of this call.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. It is a sweet call. God calls in such a way that he allures; he does not force, but draws. The freedom of the will is not taken away, but its stubbornness is conquered#footnote[#emph[Actio haec non fit coactio; Deus non torquet, sed trahit.] \[This action is not compulsion; God does not wrench, but draws.\]] (Ps 110:3): “Your people shall be willing in the day of Your power.” After this call there are no more disputes; the soul readily obeys God’s call. So when Christ called #emph[Zacchaeus,] he joyfully welcomed him into his heart and his house.#footnote[μὴ βουλομένους οὐ βιάζεται ὁ θεὸς ἀλλὰ ἕλκει. \[God does not force the unwilling, but draws them.\]]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. It is a holy call (2 Tim 1:9): “He has … called us to a holy calling.” This call of God calls men out of their sins; by it they are consecrated and set apart for God. The vessels of the tabernacle were taken from common use and set apart for a holy use. So those who are effectually called are separated from sin and consecrated to God’s service. The God whom we worship is holy, the work we are employed in is holy, and the place we hope to reach is holy; all this calls for holiness. A Christian’s heart is to be the presence chamber of the blessed Trinity; and shall not #emph[Holiness to the Lord] be written on it? Believers are children of God the Father, members of God the Son, temples of God the Holy Spirit; and shall they not be holy? Holiness is the badge and uniform of God’s people (Isa 63:18): עם־קדשך, #emph[the people of your holiness.] As chastity distinguishes a virtuous woman from a harlot, so holiness distinguishes the godly from the wicked. It is a #emph[holy calling.] Let no man who lives in sin say he is called by God. Has God called you to be a swearer, to be a drunkard? No, and let not the merely moral person say he is effectually called. What is decency without holiness? It is only a dead body strewn with flowers. The king’s picture stamped on brass will not pass as money. The merely decent man looks as if he had the image of the King of heaven stamped on him, but he is no better than counterfeit metal, which God will not accept as genuine.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. It is an irresistible call. When God calls a man by his grace, he cannot help coming. You#super[+] may resist the minister’s call, but you cannot resist the Spirit’s call. The finger of the blessed Spirit can write on a heart of stone, as he once wrote his laws on tablets of stone. God’s words are #emph[verba creativa,] creating words. When he said, “Let there be light,” there was light; and when he says, #emph[Let there be faith,] it will be so. When God called #emph[Paul,] he answered the call (Acts 26:19): “I was not disobedient to the heavenly vision”#footnote[Παῦλος μὲν ἀκούσας φωνῆς, εὐθέως ὑπήκουσε, καὶ πολλὴν ἐπεδείξατο τὴν μεταβολήν. \[Paul, when he heard the voice, obeyed at once and showed a great change.\] #emph[Chrysostom.]]. God rides out conquering in the chariot of his gospel. He makes the blind eye see and the stony heart bleed. If God will call a man, nothing will #emph[ponere obicem,] or lie in the way to hinder it. Difficulties will be untied; the powers of hell will disband (Rom 9:19): “Who can resist His will?” God bends the iron sinew and breaks down the #emph[gates of bronze] (Ps 107:16). When the Lord touches a man’s heart by his Spirit, all proud imaginations are brought down, and the royal fort of the will surrenders to God. I may allude to Ps 114:5: “What ailed you, O sea, that you fled, O Jordan, that you turned back?” The man who before was like a raging sea, foaming out wickedness, now suddenly flees back and trembles. He falls down like the #emph[jailer:] “What must I do to be saved?” What #emph[ails you, O sea?] What ails this man? The Lord has been effectually calling him; he has been working a work of grace, and now his stubborn heart is conquered by a sweet violence.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. It is a high calling (Phil 3:14): “I press on toward the goal to win the prize of God’s high calling.” It is a high calling, first, because we are called to high exercises of religion: to die to sin, to be crucified to the world, to live by faith, to #emph[have fellowship with the Father] (1 John 1:3). This is a high calling; here is a work too high for men in a state of nature to perform. Second, it is a high #emph[calling] because we are called to high privileges: to justification and adoption, to be made #emph[co-heirs with Christ]#footnote[Rom 8:17 συγκληρονόμοι #emph[Aeternae vitae & gloriae participes; aperiuntur, hic nobis limpidissimi fontes,] etc. \[Sharers of eternal life and glory; here the clearest springs are opened to us.\] Beza.]. He who is effectually called is higher than the princes of the earth.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. It is a gracious #emph[call;] it is the fruit and product of free grace. God calls some and not others: #emph[one taken, and the other left.] One is called who has a rougher, gloomier disposition, while another of sharper gifts and a sweeter temper is rejected. Here is free grace! The poor are made #emph[rich in faith,] and #emph[inherit the kingdom] (Jas 2:5), while the nobles and great ones of the world are for the most part reprobated (1 Cor 1:26): “Not many were of noble birth.” What is this but free and rich grace? Matt 11:26: “Yes, Father, for this was well-pleasing in Your sight.” In the same sermon one is effectually wrought on, and another is no more moved than a dead man by the sound of music. One hears the Spirit’s voice in the word; another does not hear it. One is softened and moistened with the influences of heaven; another, like #emph[Gideon’s dry fleece,] has no dew on him. Behold here distinguishing grace! The same affliction converts one and hardens another. To the one, affliction is like the bruising of spices, which give out a fragrant smell. To the other, it is like the pounding of weeds in a mortar, which smell worse. Where does this come from but from the εὐδοκία, the free grace of God? It is a #emph[gracious calling;] it is all enameled and interwoven with free grace. #emph[Si cogitare coeperis O anima mea, quot, & quales sint qui hanc quae tibi data est gratiam consequi non potuerunt; audisti certe quod multae gentes pertransierunt, quae omnes sine cognitione Dei in interitum sempiternum dilapsae sunt omnibus illis redemptor tuus te praetulit, gratiamque ejus largitus est; & quare hoc? Nullam praeter salvatoris Charitatem, invenire poteris Causam; elegit te in omnibus, assumpsit te ex omnibus, amavit te pre omnibus, ut memoriale ejus semper esset apud te]#footnote[#emph[Augustine.]] \[If you begin to think, O my soul, how many there are, and of what kind, who have not been able to obtain this grace that has been given to you: you have surely heard that many nations have passed away, all of which have fallen into everlasting ruin without the knowledge of God. Your Redeemer has preferred you to all of them and has given you his grace. And why this? You will find no cause except the love of the Savior. He chose you among all, took you out of all, loved you above all, so that the remembrance of him might always be with you\].]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[6\. It is a glorious call (1 Pet 5:10): “who has called you#super[+] to His eternal glory.” We are called to the glorious enjoyment of the ever-blessed God. It is as if a man were called out of a prison to sit on a throne. #emph[Quintus Curtius] writes of one who was digging in his garden when he was called to be king. So God calls us #emph[to glory and virtue] (2 Pet 1:3): first to virtue, then to glory. At #emph[Athens] there were two temples, the temple of virtue and the temple of honor, and no man could go into the temple of honor except through the temple of virtue. So God calls us first to #emph[virtue] and then to #emph[glory.] What is glory among men, which most people hunt after so, but a feather blown in the air? What is it compared with the #emph[weight of glory?] Is there not great reason why we should follow God’s call? He calls us to promotion; can there be any loss or harm in this? God would have us part with nothing for him except what will damn us if we keep it. He has no design on us but to make us happy. He calls us to salvation; he calls us to a kingdom. Oh, how we should then, with #emph[Bartimaeus,] throw off our ragged coat of sin and follow Christ when he calls!]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[7\. It is a rare call. Only a few are savingly called (Matt 22:14): “Few are chosen.” Few, not #emph[taken all together,] but #emph[by comparison.] The word ἐκκαλεῖν, to #emph[call,] signifies to choose some out from among others. Many have the light brought to them, but few have their eyes anointed to see that light (Rev 3:4): “You do have ὀλίγα ὀνόματα, a few people in Sardis who have not soiled their garments.” The devil has the vintage; God has only a few gleanings. How many millions sit in #emph[the region of darkness!] And in those climates where the Sun of righteousness does shine, there are many who receive the #emph[light] of the truth, but not the #emph[love] of it. There are many formalists, but few believers. There is something that looks like faith which is not faith. The #emph[Cyprian] diamond (says #emph[Pliny]) sparkles like the true diamond, but it is not of the right kind; it will break under the hammer. So the hypocrite’s faith will break under the hammer of persecution. Only a few are truly called. Precious stones are few compared with the number of pebbles. Most men shape their religion to the fashion of the times; they are for the music and the idol (Dan 3:7). Serious thought about this would make us #emph[work out our salvation with fear,] and labor to be among those few whom #emph[God] has brought into a state of grace.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[8\. It is an unchangeable call (Rom 11:29): “God’s gifts and His call are χαρίσματα ἀμεταμέλητα, without repentance”; that is (as a learned writer says), those gifts that flow from election#footnote[#emph[Willet.]]. When #emph[God] calls a man, he does not repent of it. #emph[God] does not do as many friends do, love one day and hate the next; or as princes do, who make their subjects favorites and then throw them into prison. This is the blessedness of a saint: his condition admits of no change. #emph[God’s call] is founded on his #emph[decree,] and his decree is unchangeable. Acts of grace cannot be reversed. God blots out his people’s #emph[sins,] but not their #emph[names.] Let the world ring its changes every hour; a believer’s condition is fixed and unalterable.]]

=== Section VI. Showing the End of Effectual Calling.

6\. #smallcaps[The] end of our #emph[heavenly calling] is the honor of the most high God (Eph 1:12): “that we … would be for the praise of His glory.” He who is in the state of nature is no more fit to honor God than a beast is to perform acts of reason. A man before conversion continually brings dishonor on God. Black vapors rise out of fens and marshy ground and cloud and darken the sun. So black vapors of sin rise out of the natural man’s heart and cast a cloud on God’s glory. The sinner is well versed in treason, but understands nothing of loyalty to the King of heaven. Yes, but there are some on whom the lot of free grace falls. These will be taken like jewels from among the rubbish and be #emph[effectually called,] so that they may lift up God’s name and honor in the world. The Lord will have some in every age who will oppose the corruptions of the times, bear witness to his truths, and turn sinners from the error of their ways. He will have his mighty men, as King #emph[David] had. Those who have been monuments of God’s mercy will be trumpets of his praise.
